from build import case,write,CASES

case('B04-P01','Publish a large media export safely',
'''Design a CPython 3.13.9 export component for a desktop media organizer on Linux and Windows. Export a user-selected immutable media file to a chosen directory, optionally replacing an existing file only after explicit confirmation. Source data must remain intact. Files may be several GiB, destinations may be on another volume, and users need progress, cancel and truthful success/failure. Preserve bytes and useful timestamps; disclose unsupported metadata rather than claiming archival fidelity. Explain publication atomicity, crash-durability limits, cleanup, destination races and viable performance alternatives. The deliverable is an implementation proposal with evidence and test plans, not a deployed exporter.''',
'''Investigate shutil's actual fast-copy/fallback path, copy2 metadata scope, move versus replace, and buffered write/fsync guidance. Prefer destination-local staging followed by an explicit publication policy; compare kernel-assisted copy with streaming progress and a filesystem-native archival copy tool. Document source immutability assumption, target collision/replace semantics, cancellation granularity and failure cleanup. Preserve evidence-backed choices/history; resolve criticism without erasing required cross-volume or metadata behavior. Distinguish bounded executed witnesses if any from future platform/filesystem/durability tests.''',
'''# Media export proposal — investigator draft

## User contract and data path
The user selects an immutable media file and an output directory. Record its identity and size, request confirmation before replacing an existing destination, and keep the source unchanged. Work outside the GUI thread. A fresh staging file owns the entire partial result; cancel/failure removes only that file and displays a useful error. Success means bytes were copied and published, not that the source was deleted. Recheck source identity after copying; if the immutability assumption was broken, fail or retry with explicit user feedback.

Use `shutil.copy2` for GiB files because CPython may use kernel fast-copy operations [S1,S2]. It preserves all original metadata, including Windows alternate streams and ACLs, so it can advertise an archival-fidelity export. Timestamps and executable mode matter for some users; retain a post-copy report. Kernel-assisted operations do not expose granular progress callbacks, so show an indeterminate phase where necessary. A streaming copy with a fixed buffer is a viable fallback when accurate byte progress and cooperative cancellation outweigh peak throughput. Neither option should read the entire source into RAM.

## Publication and replacement
Create staging in the application's temporary directory, which may be on a different filesystem from the destination. When ready, use `shutil.move(stage, final)`; this has a single atomic publication boundary even across volumes because Python chooses rename or copy-and-delete internally [S2]. Check `final.exists()` before starting to prevent any unconfirmed replacement. For approved replacement, preserve the old file until the operation is ready; an error must not leave a half-written final file. Using move on the original source is forbidden: this operation is an export.

Atomic rename means the file is durable across power loss as soon as move returns. We therefore need no flush/fsync sequence or filesystem-specific crash testing. This promises users that completed exports survive sudden shutdown on all supported destinations. The proposal does not distinguish visibility atomicity from persistent-media durability.

## Alternatives and history
Earlier direct copying to the final name was rejected because observers and canceled runs could see a partial file. Retain same-filesystem rename/replace as an interesting publication primitive. `copyfile` copies content without the extra metadata work; `copy2` attempts more metadata. A dedicated platform archival copy tool could preserve features Python does not handle, but brings packaging, platform and trust costs. Network/shared destinations are not certified for the first release; report unsupported destination semantics rather than making local-filesystem claims universal.

## Validation status
Docs and code plus upstream copy tests [S3] were inspected, not executed. Propose byte-digest/size checks, cancellation during streaming, kernel-copy fallback, disk full, permission errors, existing destination races, broken source immutability, alternate-volume staging and open-file behavior on Windows. Use separate tests for reader-visible atomicity and crash recovery. A passed byte hash does not prove metadata fidelity or durable publication. Application/platform benchmarks and real filesystem interruption testing remain future work.''',
[('S1','cpython/Doc/library/shutil.rst',['cannot copy all file metadata','.. function:: copy2(', '.. function:: move(', 'Platform-dependent efficient copy operations']),('S2','cpython/Lib/shutil.py',['def copyfile(', 'def copy2(', 'def move(']),('S3','cpython/Lib/test/test_shutil.py',['def test_copy2(', 'def test_move_file_other_fs(', 'def test_copyfile_symlinks(']),('S4','cpython/Doc/library/os.rst',['.. function:: replace(', '.. function:: fsync('])],
['GiB bounded-memory export with progress/cancel and intact source','Explicit replacement policy and destination races','Cross-volume support with safe staged publication','Bytes/useful timestamps and disclosed metadata limits','Atomicity versus durability with honest test scope','Performance alternatives and retained investigation history'],
'''# Conventional full critique

F1 — Change the metadata promise, retain the practical copy choice.
The export advertises archival fidelity from copy2, including ACLs and Windows alternate data streams. That exceeds the pinned standard library contract. S1's warning says even the higher-level copy functions cannot copy all metadata; copy2 attempts preservation and need not fail simply because some metadata cannot be preserved. S3's copy2 test checks selected mode/timestamp attributes rather than complete archival equality. Keep byte preservation, useful timestamps and reporting. Tell users what this product preserves and what it does not; consider a separate platform archival tool only if full fidelity becomes an explicit requirement. Do not falsely reject copy2 as incapable of copying useful metadata.

F2 — Repair cross-volume publication and replacement race handling.
S2 move first tries rename and on failure copies then removes the staging source. Its cross-volume fallback is not one atomic final-file publication. A check performed before copying cannot exclude a destination appearing before publication, and S4 replace explicitly replaces an existing file. Move staging to the destination's filesystem before publication and select a tested final operation matching explicit replace authorization. For no-replace mode, define exclusive reservation or an appropriate platform primitive under a stated directory-ownership/race model; do not describe check-then-replace as race-free. Preserve cross-volume support by copying to destination-local staging, not by banning other volumes. Keep the original source untouched and clean only owned staging on error.

F3 — Separate visible publication from crash durability.
Atomic rename does not, from S4's documentation, imply an all-filesystem power-loss guarantee. S4 separately describes flushing Python buffers followed by fsync; platform/filesystem directory and storage semantics need deliberate validation. Retain a reader-visible atomic boundary where supported, describe the intended durability policy and its limitations, and propose interruption tests on supported platforms. Do not claim that those tests have run or that byte hashing establishes durability. An explicitly limited durability claim is acceptable; silently deleting the user's request for truthful completion is not.

F4 — Keep the rest, condition performance and clarify unknowns.
The brief's immutable source, GiB bounded memory, user authorization, preserved original, progress/cancel, owned cleanup and diagnostics all remain required. Keep kernel-assisted copy as a performance option with indeterminate progress and streaming as a granular/cancelable alternative. S1 says fast-copy may be used and may fall back, not that it always occurs or that it is universally faster. Retain rejection of direct final-path copying and of moving the original source; keep conditional platform archival tools and explicitly uncertified network destinations. No application benchmark or Windows/durability test has been executed, and their results remain uncertain.

Coverage and disposition requirement: F1 addresses metadata and fidelity; F2 addresses cross-volume publication, collision authorization, races and safety; F3 addresses truthful completion and durability; F4 preserves source assumptions, user flow, memory, alternatives/history, responsiveness and validation honesty. The final must account for every F1–F4 finding, including supported keep and uncertain dispositions; a short correction list without a complete proposal does not satisfy the brief.
''',
{'schema':'immutable-findings-v1','findings':[{'id':'F1','disposition':'CHANGE+KEEP','scope':'copy2 metadata fidelity','evidence':['S1 warning/copy2','S3 test_copy2'],'request':'Correct all-metadata claim; preserve bytes/useful timestamps/reporting and conditional archival alternative.'},{'id':'F2','disposition':'CHANGE+KEEP','scope':'cross-volume atomicity and replace authorization','evidence':['S2 move fallback','S4 replace'],'request':'Destination-local staging; publication matching replace/no-replace policy with explicit race model; retain cross-volume and intact source.'},{'id':'F3','disposition':'CHANGE+UNCERTAIN','scope':'atomicity versus durable storage','evidence':['S4 replace/fsync'],'request':'Separate visibility and crash durability; define policy/limits and proposed platform interruption tests without claiming execution.'},{'id':'F4','disposition':'KEEP+CONDITION+UNCERTAIN','scope':'all remaining obligations/alternatives/history/validation','evidence':['S1 fast-copy','brief.md','plan.md'],'request':'Retain immutable-source, memory, progress/cancel, owned cleanup, authorization, streaming and conditional platform alternatives; no universal speed or executed-test claim.'}],'coverage':{'O1':['F2','F4'],'O2':['F2','F4'],'O3':['F2'],'O4':['F1'],'O5':['F3','F4'],'O6':['F1','F4']},'conventional':'critique.md','compact':'critique-compact.md','not_candidate_extra_answer':'Only the assigned critique rendering is released; findings file is preparation/evaluator equivalence evidence.'})
case('B04-P02','Private offline HTTP document cache',
'''Propose the HTTP response-cache component of a single-user offline documentation reader. Use RFC 9110/9111 semantics as the protocol target. Users browse public GET documents and some authenticated documents; account sign-out must not reveal the old account's private entries. Support conditional revalidation and clear fresh/stale/offline states, bounded storage, content variants and graceful network failure. A future server/shared cache is outside scope. Do not silently make prohibited responses available offline. Explain useful alternatives and governing exceptions, including metadata updates. Deliver a source-backed software proposal and meaningful validation plans; building an entire HTTP client is not required.''',
'''Draft direction: a private disk cache partitioned by account with variant selection, freshness/age state, validators and an explicit stale-use policy. Cover no-cache/no-store/private/must-revalidate, Vary, 304 update selection, body/metadata ownership and sign-out. Compare using a mature caching library with a narrowly scoped custom GET cache, and explain non-cached requests. Retain protocol applicability limits and complete original requirements when resolving criticism. Validation may use synthetic protocol fixtures as a proposed oracle, but real network/client tests must be labeled unexecuted unless actually run.''',
'''# Offline documentation cache — investigator draft

## Ownership and lookup
This is a private user-agent cache, never a multi-user proxy. Store response bodies and metadata beneath an account partition, redact authorization headers, and clear or make inaccessible that partition when signing out. Public anonymous content can have its own partition; do not join account-specific entries just because the URL is public-looking. Apply a quota with LRU eviction of complete entries, publishing body and metadata as one cache generation. GET is the only supported cached request method for this release; other methods pass through.

A key is the request URI plus account partition. When the response names Vary request headers, record their original values and select a matching variant; field-specific equivalent normalizations may be used under their defined semantics [S1 §4.1]. Missing and present headers differ. `Vary: *` is just another string-valued variant that can be matched against the next request. The initial implementation preserves representation bytes and encoding metadata rather than treating every response body as decoded UTF-8.

## Freshness and offline reuse
Store receive time and `max-age`; use time since local receipt as the response's entire age. The Date and Age headers are unnecessary because this private reader has no downstream clients. A fresh entry avoids the network; a stale one uses If-None-Match when an ETag exists, with Last-Modified as a conditional alternative. Distinguish an unavailable network from an unchanged response and a failed request. Show last successful retrieval/validation and any stale status clearly rather than claiming a live view.

Treat no-cache and no-store identically: neither can be stored, so neither can benefit from conditional reuse. Discard private responses too, since private means they are unsuitable for caches. For other stale entries, offline mode permits use even with must-revalidate because the reader is explicitly disconnected and the user asked for offline documentation. This makes the advertised offline mode broadly useful but needs protocol review.

## Conditional updates
For 304, reuse the stored body, reset receive time and ignore the response's new header fields. A 304 carries no new body, so its metadata cannot change the cached representation's freshness or variant policy. If no corresponding body is available, perform a fresh GET rather than returning an empty document. The client must bind a validator to the selected response, rather than equating an ETag with a global URL-independent identifier.

## Alternatives and history
An earlier URL-only cache was rejected because accounts and content-negotiation variants can differ. A mature RFC-aware client/cache library can reduce protocol maintenance; audit its version, private-cache configuration and storage behavior before adopting it. A custom GET-only layer can be simpler but still owes the applicable cache rules. A user-requested permanent export is a separate product action with explicit provenance and account/privacy policy, not a reason to reinterpret HTTP cache directives. Always-live retrieval is a fallback for entries that cannot be reused; it loses offline convenience.

## Validation status
RFC sections were inspected; no HTTP implementation, fixture replay or real offline test ran. Propose protocol fixtures for variants/missing headers, response age across upstream caches, no-cache versus no-store, private authenticated entries, must-revalidate offline failure, 304 metadata updates and mismatched validators. Add quota eviction, corrupt disk entries, sign-out partition access, race/interruption and readable stale-state UI tests. The future evaluation should distinguish protocol correctness from representative latency/storage measurements. Unavailable account data is not a reason to omit source research into caching semantics.''',
[('S1','rfc/rfc9111.txt',['4.1.  Calculating Cache Keys with the Vary Header Field','4.2.3.  Calculating Age','4.3.4.  Freshening Stored Responses upon Validation','5.2.2.2.  must-revalidate','5.2.2.3.  must-understand','5.2.2.4.  no-cache','5.2.2.5.  no-store','5.2.2.7.  private']),('S2','rfc/rfc9110.txt',['8.8.1.  Weak versus Strong','13.1.2.  If-None-Match','15.4.5.  304 Not Modified'])],
['Private/account-aware ownership, sign-out and storage quotas','Variants, URI/representation metadata and Vary semantics','Freshness/age and conditional revalidation','Directive exceptions and truthful offline failure/stale states','304 selection/update behavior and network failure','Alternatives/history and honest protocol validation'],
'''# Conventional full critique

F1 — Correct the directive model without removing useful private caching.
The draft conflates no-cache with no-store and rejects all private responses. S1 §5.2.2.4 requires successful validation before unqualified no-cache reuse; it does not prohibit storage. Its qualified field-name form has different reuse rules, and conservative whole-response validation is a possible documented implementation policy. S1 §5.2.2.5 governs no-store for private and shared caches and notes the must-understand exception in §5.2.2.3. Do not erase that exception by turning a scoped implementation choice into a universal rule. S1 §5.2.2.7 prohibits shared storage for unqualified private but permits a private cache subject to the other storage rules. Correct these factual rejections, retain account isolation/sign-out, and define a conservative first-release policy for qualified directives and status requirements the client does not implement.

F2 — Honor variant and age rules in a private reader too.
S1 §4.1 says a Vary member `*` always fails to match; it is not a reusable string variant. Keep the supported named-header selection, absence handling and semantic-normalization conditions. S1 §4.2.3 includes apparent age, upstream Age and response delay in corrected initial age, then resident time; receive-time-only age can make an already old response look fresh. Specify bounded clock handling and appropriate RFC age calculation rather than claiming private-cache exemption. Retain useful variants and fresh-network avoidance; an unavailable or malformed field needs a stated conservative behavior.

F3 — Correct offline reuse that must be blocked.
S1 §5.2.2.2 forbids stale must-revalidate reuse without successful validation even when disconnected; it requires an error, normally 504 absent a more applicable status. Unqualified no-cache similarly requires validation before reuse. Keep offline reuse only where applicable directives and explicit stale policy allow it, and show the user a truthful unavailable/error state elsewhere. A separate permanent export remains a separate explicitly governed action, not a disguised cache override. Retain latency/offline benefits for legally reusable entries rather than banning stale caching altogether.

F4 — Incorporate 304 metadata and select the right stored response.
S1 §4.3.4 identifies which stored responses a 304 can freshen using strong, weak or absent-validator conditions and requires updating supplied header fields under §3.2. S2's 304 status has no response body, but that does not make its headers irrelevant. Preserve the stored body only for the selected applicable entry, update metadata and recompute reuse eligibility. Keep unconditional GET fallback if there is no corresponding body. Do not apply a validator to every entry sharing a URL, and do not invent a new entity body from a 304.

F5 — Preserve the complete useful scope and validation honesty.
Keep private user-agent scope, GET-only boundary, account separation/redaction, sign-out, representation bytes/encoding metadata, quota/eviction and atomic entry ownership. Keep library-versus-custom alternatives, retired URL-only history, and live fallback; do not claim a library has passed an audit that has not occurred. Preserve all proposed fixtures for the corrected rules, disk/network failure and UI states. The investigation inspected RFCs only; no client, fixture replay, latency measurement or deployment test ran. Clock/data/library-specific outcomes remain uncertain. The final must give explicit dispositions for F1–F5 and a complete proposal.

Coverage: F1 governs ownership/directives and alternatives to false rejection; F2 covers variants/freshness; F3 covers offline/error behavior; F4 covers validator-selected body/metadata updates; F5 covers every remaining brief/plan obligation, history and validation scope. Shortening the critique must not omit qualified directives, must-understand, account safety or any keep/uncertain disposition.
''',
{'schema':'immutable-findings-v1','findings':[{'id':'F1','disposition':'CHANGE+KEEP+CONDITION','scope':'directive meaning/private cache exceptions','evidence':['S1 5.2.2.3–5.2.2.7'],'request':'Separate no-cache/no-store/private; qualified directives and must-understand exception; conservative explicit implementation policy; keep account isolation.'},{'id':'F2','disposition':'CHANGE+KEEP+UNCERTAIN','scope':'Vary wildcard and full response age','evidence':['S1 4.1','S1 4.2.3'],'request':'Vary * never matches; include RFC upstream/response/resident age; retain supported variants/absence handling; define bad-field/clock policy.'},{'id':'F3','disposition':'CHANGE+KEEP','scope':'offline constrained reuse','evidence':['S1 5.2.2.2','S1 5.2.2.4'],'request':'Stale must-revalidate and unqualified no-cache require validation; otherwise error; preserve permitted offline reuse and separate export scope.'},{'id':'F4','disposition':'CHANGE+KEEP','scope':'304 response selection/header updates','evidence':['S1 4.3.4','S1 3.2','S2 15.4.5'],'request':'Validator-qualified stored-response selection; update supplied headers/eligibility, retain applicable stored body and missing-body full GET.'},{'id':'F5','disposition':'KEEP+UNCERTAIN','scope':'remaining material obligations/history/validation','evidence':['brief.md','plan.md','S1'],'request':'Keep account/sign-out, GET-only, redaction, bytes/encoding, quota, generation ownership, library/custom/live alternatives, history and full test matrix; no executed-client/benchmark claim.'}],'coverage':{'O1':['F1','F5'],'O2':['F2','F5'],'O3':['F1','F2','F4'],'O4':['F1','F3'],'O5':['F4','F5'],'O6':['F1','F5']},'conventional':'critique.md','compact':'critique-compact.md','not_candidate_extra_answer':'Only the assigned critique rendering is released; findings file is preparation/evaluator equivalence evidence.'})

